.TH CHTTPSVR_ENGINE_WAIT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_engine_wait \- wait for the engine thread to exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_engine_wait(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks the calling thread until chttpserver's own shared reactor has
fully stopped; whether a stop was already triggered before this call,
is triggered concurrently, or is not triggered until sometime after
this call (this blocks indefinitely if nothing ever stops the reactor,
e.g. an external SIGTERM handler calling chttpsvr_engine_stop() at some
later, unknown time).

Dropping the last reference to this reactor (destroying the last
chttpsvr via __chttpsvr_destroy) triggers an asynchronous teardown that
does not itself block the caller that triggered it; this function is
the explicit escape hatch for programs that need to synchronise with
that shutdown, or with an externally signalled chttpsvr_engine_stop()
call.  This reactor is independent of chttpclient's own engine, so this
function has no relationship to chttpclient's async work in the same
process.

Safe to call concurrently from multiple threads; each blocks until
the reactor is confirmed fully stopped, regardless of which caller's
release or chttpsvr_engine_stop() call was the one that triggered it.

Calling this from within a request handler or middleware currently
running on ANY chttpsvr instance's own worker pool is a FATAL error
(fatal_err(), abort()/SIGABRT), reported immediately rather than
hanging: draining that server's worker pool can never complete while
this exact in-flight request is itself blocked here waiting for the
engine to finish exiting, deadlocking the entire shared engine's
shutdown, not just one server. An admin/shutdown endpoint that wants
to fully drain before responding should call chttpsvr_engine_stop()
and simply return instead; call this function from a separate thread.

After this function returns, the engine logger (see
chttpsvr_set_engine_logger) has been closed; if engine-level logging is
wanted again after a subsequent chttpsvr_start, chttpsvr_set_engine_logger
must be called again.

Safe to call before the reactor has ever started (no-op).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR __chttpsvr_destroy (3),
.BR chttpsvr_set_engine_logger (3)
